WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.730
Imagine you and I both use an app to find
an apartment.

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.710
Yesterday you could get a place. Today the
listings on your screen are identical,

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:08.636
but the app has changed what it shows me.
Could that cost you the apartment?

00:00:09.423 --> 00:00:11.750
That's the question we tested. Here's what
happened.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.130
In our New York rental simulation,

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:19.730
some renters had one to five feasible
apartments. The app showed them every one.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:22.149
We compared two ways of recommending
apartments to the rest of the market.

00:00:23.341 --> 00:00:26.081
With a rule that could replace at most one
suggestion per renter,

00:00:26.081 --> 00:00:27.992
13.1 percent of this group got an
apartment.

00:00:29.185 --> 00:00:32.624
With balanced shortlists across the
market, 27.5 percent got one.

00:00:33.817 --> 00:00:36.657
The rate more than doubled. The same
people saw the same apartments,

00:00:36.657 --> 00:00:38.717
and each still faced the same landlord
priorities.

00:00:38.717 --> 00:00:39.964
Only their competition changed.

00:00:40.836 --> 00:00:44.296
Here the group consists of 2,328 renter
appearances in our September

00:00:44.296 --> 00:00:47.024
and October enriched markets. These are
simulated assignments.

00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:51.137
That sounds like a lot to attribute to
somebody else's screen.

00:00:51.137 --> 00:00:52.750
A two-seat example shows how it can
happen.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.850
Let's start with two applicants and two
school seats.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:01.730
A prefers school x to school y. B accepts
only x.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:03.531
And school x gives A priority over B.

00:01:04.858 --> 00:01:08.018
At first, A sees y and B sees x. Both get
a seat.

00:01:09.345 --> 00:01:13.013
Now change only A's recommendation. Show A
school x instead.

00:01:14.339 --> 00:01:18.159
Both applicants still see just one option.
But now they compete for the same seat.

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:20.251
A gets x, B gets nothing, and y sits
empty.

00:01:21.578 --> 00:01:24.338
Imagine asking each person to review what
the app showed them.

00:01:24.338 --> 00:01:27.738
A can say the suggestion improved. B can
say the suggestion stayed the same.

00:01:27.738 --> 00:01:30.026
Put those answers together and you still
haven't counted the lost seat.

00:01:30.964 --> 00:01:32.750
To see it, we have to run the assignment.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:38.130
This example gives us a way to evaluate a
recommender.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.390
First we ask whether the shortlist
includes a reference option.

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:41.372
We call that coverage.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.720
For rentals, the reference is the
apartment a renter would be assigned

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.540
if everyone saw every feasible listing. We
measure rental coverage

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.724
among people who get an apartment in that
full-list assignment.

00:01:50.552 --> 00:01:52.432
In Chile we check whether a family's list
includes

00:01:52.432 --> 00:01:53.937
the school where the child later enrolled.

00:01:55.044 --> 00:01:57.104
Then we ask who gets a place.

00:01:57.104 --> 00:01:59.684
This time we run the assignment using
everyone's shortlists together.

00:01:59.684 --> 00:02:01.704
Placement counts a person who gets any
seat or apartment,

00:02:01.704 --> 00:02:03.077
even if it differs from the reference.

00:02:04.184 --> 00:02:06.724
Finally, we pair the two runs person by
person.

00:02:06.724 --> 00:02:09.824
Someone placed under the old policy and
unplaced under the new one is an exit.

00:02:09.824 --> 00:02:12.144
For someone who gets a place for the first
time, we count an entry.

00:02:12.973 --> 00:02:15.750
Two policies can place the same number of
people and still produce both.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:21.730
Housing and school admissions let us ask
the same question with different evidence.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:24.730
For rentals we have actual listings and
survey-based household profiles.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:27.016
Preferences, landlord priorities and
assignments are modeled.

00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:31.523
We reconstructed 1,280 markets. Each
comparison uses the same

00:02:31.523 --> 00:02:34.251
feasible apartments, renters and priority
draws under both policies.

00:02:35.235 --> 00:02:37.715
We use ordinary sampling and an enriched
design with more affordable

00:02:37.715 --> 00:02:40.855
listings and more renters who have
options. That distinction matters.

00:02:40.855 --> 00:02:43.313
Some markets could not be built under the
Staten Island supply requirements.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:45.900
A household with no feasible apartment
cannot

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.356
gain access through a better ranking
alone.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:51.060
Chile gives us families' submitted
rankings and an admissions mechanism we

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:56.060
can replay. In the 2021 entry grades, only
6,133 out of roughly two hundred thousand

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:58.226
linked pupils received a different list
under the two policies.

00:02:58.993 --> 00:03:03.091
Yet 237 of 309 net lost placements fell
among pupils with unchanged lists.

00:03:04.075 --> 00:03:06.195
Most pupils were in that group to begin
with.

00:03:06.195 --> 00:03:08.295
The point is what an audit would miss if
it followed only people whose

00:03:08.295 --> 00:03:10.982
recommendations changed. These new
assignments are counterfactual.

00:03:11.749 --> 00:03:14.750
The families supplied the rankings; our
comparison changed the menus.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:20.510
The first rule coordinates one option. We
call it the anchor.

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:22.750
Find a maximum matching across all
feasible pairs.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.794
When several maximum matchings exist,
prefer pairs already on the shortlists.

00:03:26.564 --> 00:03:28.964
Then put each assigned option onto that
person's list,

00:03:28.964 --> 00:03:30.185
replacing at most one option.

00:03:31.175 --> 00:03:33.415
The second rule coordinates the whole
shortlist.

00:03:33.415 --> 00:03:35.195
Count how often each listing is
recommended,

00:03:35.195 --> 00:03:36.995
and minimize the sum of those counts
squared.

00:03:36.995 --> 00:03:39.307
Concentrating recommendations on a few
listings becomes expensive.

00:03:40.077 --> 00:03:43.617
Among equally balanced solutions, favor
the saved GPT ranks.

00:03:43.617 --> 00:03:45.291
We solve this using standard min-cost
flow.

00:03:46.281 --> 00:03:48.881
The first recommendation to a listing
costs one unit.

00:03:48.881 --> 00:03:50.521
The next costs three, then five.

00:03:50.521 --> 00:03:52.198
Those are the differences between
successive squares.

00:03:52.968 --> 00:03:55.808
If two feasible apartments are equally
useful for our tie breaker,

00:03:55.808 --> 00:03:58.248
the algorithm has a reason to recommend
the less crowded one.

00:03:58.248 --> 00:03:59.468
Nobody has reserved it.

00:03:59.468 --> 00:04:01.825
Applicants still compete according to
preferences and priorities.

00:04:02.815 --> 00:04:05.055
Neither rule sees the realized preference
shocks,

00:04:05.055 --> 00:04:06.795
landlord rankings or reference
assignments.

00:04:06.795 --> 00:04:08.636
They use feasibility and saved
recommendations.

00:04:09.626 --> 00:04:11.786
There are two different matchings in this
experiment.

00:04:11.786 --> 00:04:14.026
The anchor matching tells the platform
which suggestions to add.

00:04:14.026 --> 00:04:15.286
It is a planning calculation.

00:04:15.286 --> 00:04:16.976
Then applicants try their favorite visible
option.

00:04:17.746 --> 00:04:21.266
Each listing tentatively holds the
highest-priority applicant it can accept.

00:04:21.266 --> 00:04:23.446
Anyone turned away tries the next
apartment on their list.

00:04:23.446 --> 00:04:25.322
This is applicant-proposing deferred
acceptance.

00:04:26.092 --> 00:04:28.272
The person the planner had in mind can
still lose that apartment in

00:04:28.272 --> 00:04:31.127
the assignment. That gap is exactly where
a retention question arises.

00:04:32.117 --> 00:04:34.497
Both keep each person's list length fixed.

00:04:34.497 --> 00:04:37.597
But balancing can replace several options,
while the anchor replaces at most one.

00:04:37.597 --> 00:04:39.750
Neither rule explicitly protects existing
recipients.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.930
The graph puts four policies on the same
axes.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:46.750
Right means better coverage of the
full-list reference,

00:04:46.750 --> 00:04:48.410
and up means more people getting an
apartment.

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:51.081
Each dot is a mean for the same September
and October enriched markets.

00:04:52.135 --> 00:04:55.795
Start at random shortlists. GPT moves
right and a little down.

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:58.675
It found more reference apartments, but
fewer people were placed. Adding one

00:04:58.675 --> 00:05:01.696
coordinated option moves the GPT point
upward with little change in coverage.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:05.570
Now compare balancing with anchoring.
Balancing gains about thirteen

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:07.906
points of placement and gives up about
three points of reference coverage.

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:10.908
Both differences keep the same sign across
the three calendar

00:05:10.908 --> 00:05:14.008
windows under both sampling designs, even
using intervals adjusted

00:05:14.008 --> 00:05:16.356
across the primary comparisons within each
design and window.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:20.570
But move your finger back to the random
dot and compare it with balancing.

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:21.870
Now both outcomes improve.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:24.221
Whether there is a tradeoff depends on
which policies you're choosing between.

00:05:25.275 --> 00:05:28.275
We also tried giving random shortlists the
same anchoring rule.

00:05:28.275 --> 00:05:31.895
Anchored GPT placed just seven more renter
appearances out of 10,240.

00:05:31.895 --> 00:05:33.395
The paired placement interval includes
zero.

00:05:34.197 --> 00:05:36.417
And a score-based benchmark with extra
information from

00:05:36.417 --> 00:05:39.037
the simulator does better on both outcomes
than balanced GPT.

00:05:39.037 --> 00:05:40.891
The comparison supports coordinating
recommendations.

00:05:41.693 --> 00:05:43.750
It leaves plenty of room for a simpler way
to rank them.

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:48.690
Return to the renters from the opening
slide.

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:50.230
Balancing changed what other people saw,

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:52.250
while this group kept all its feasible
listings.

00:05:52.250 --> 00:05:53.583
How did its match rate more than double?

00:05:54.859 --> 00:05:56.899
In the September and October enriched
sample,

00:05:56.899 --> 00:05:59.679
304 of these renter appearances started
with a match under the anchor.

00:05:59.679 --> 00:06:02.159
Under balancing, 349 others gained one.

00:06:02.159 --> 00:06:03.977
But fifteen who used to have an apartment
lost theirs.

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.490
That leaves 638 matched under balancing.

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:09.991
Those fifteen losses disappear if all we
report is the average gain.

00:06:11.267 --> 00:06:13.247
The counts pool the market appearances.

00:06:13.247 --> 00:06:15.847
The displayed rates instead average within
simulation seeds.

00:06:15.847 --> 00:06:18.067
The estimated gain was about fourteen and
a half percentage points,

00:06:18.067 --> 00:06:20.666
with a pointwise interval from about 13.3
to 15.5.

00:06:21.942 --> 00:06:23.902
We repeated this comparison in the other
calendar windows

00:06:23.902 --> 00:06:26.882
and under ordinary sampling. Across all
three enriched windows,

00:06:26.882 --> 00:06:29.240
the unchanged-list subgroup gained about
fourteen to fifteen points.

00:06:30.153 --> 00:06:33.613
Under ordinary sampling, it gained about
twenty-one to twenty-five points.

00:06:33.613 --> 00:06:35.853
These are six comparisons within one
modeling framework.

00:06:35.853 --> 00:06:38.389
Some listings recur across windows and we
had already studied those windows.

00:06:39.302 --> 00:06:41.750
They are a check on the assumptions under
which the pattern appears.

00:06:44.450 --> 00:06:45.810
Consider the jobs replication.

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:48.610
We use New York metro postings and
survey-based seeker profiles.

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:50.922
In each simulated market, people see up to
five feasible jobs.

00:06:51.664 --> 00:06:54.644
The experiment pairs the same seekers and
postings across policies.

00:06:54.644 --> 00:06:56.269
The outcome is the assignment in that
simulation.

00:06:57.203 --> 00:07:00.863
With GPT shortlists, 63.5 percent get a
placement.

00:07:00.863 --> 00:07:04.563
Coordinate just one option on each list,
and that rises to 79.4 percent.

00:07:04.563 --> 00:07:08.137
That is 612 additional placements on net
across 3,840 seeker appearances.

00:07:08.879 --> 00:07:11.539
Then apply the same anchoring rule to
random shortlists.

00:07:11.539 --> 00:07:14.659
That simple control reaches 81.4 percent.
The improvement over plain

00:07:14.659 --> 00:07:17.229
GPT also shows up when the anchor starts
with random suggestions.

00:07:17.971 --> 00:07:20.908
This comparison gives us no placement
advantage to claim for the language model.

00:07:21.842 --> 00:07:24.502
Here is what we can learn even with only
the published totals.

00:07:24.502 --> 00:07:26.722
The anchor changed 920 personal lists.

00:07:26.722 --> 00:07:28.482
Under the fixed priorities in this jobs
model,

00:07:28.482 --> 00:07:30.685
changing one list can create at most one
new recipient.

00:07:31.427 --> 00:07:34.147
To see why, remove that seeker, then add
them back.

00:07:34.147 --> 00:07:37.147
Rejections travel down a single chain.
Several people can switch jobs,

00:07:37.147 --> 00:07:39.163
but only one person at a time is looking
for a vacancy.

00:07:40.097 --> 00:07:43.197
So there can be at most 920 entrants
across these edits.

00:07:43.197 --> 00:07:45.937
Entrants minus exits equals the net gain
of 612.

00:07:45.937 --> 00:07:48.077
Subtraction gives an upper bound of 308
exits,

00:07:48.077 --> 00:07:49.707
and zero is also consistent with the
bounds.

00:07:50.449 --> 00:07:52.149
We cannot tell which individuals lost a
match

00:07:52.149 --> 00:07:54.449
or measure coverage after the anchor from
these totals.

00:07:54.449 --> 00:07:56.750
Chile's individual application records let
us follow that question further.

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:01.390
In Chile we can follow individual records.

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:04.470
We compare two classifiers trained on an
earlier cohort to predict initial offers.

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:05.970
One uses where a family ranked each
school,

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:07.247
and the other leaves that information out.

00:08:08.197 --> 00:08:09.717
Both choose shortlists;

00:08:09.717 --> 00:08:12.417
then we rerun admissions using the same
families' submitted preferences.

00:08:12.417 --> 00:08:14.781
We also score whether the school of later
enrollment appears on each list.

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:19.710
The rank-informed policy gains 4,638
enrollment hits overall,

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:22.970
but loses 1,670 placements compared with
the rank-blind policy.

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:24.091
Which families bear the loss?

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:28.120
Look at pupils who had no initial offer in
the historical record.

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:30.360
They account for 7.4 percent of linked
applicant-years,

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:32.025
but 76 percent of the net placement
decline.

00:08:32.974 --> 00:08:35.954
For every hundred records in the study
only about seven belong to this group.

00:08:35.954 --> 00:08:37.791
Yet about three quarters of the total net
loss falls there.

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:43.300
In counts, that is 1,270 out of 1,670 net
lost placements.

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:45.540
We compare two simulated assignments for
the same pupils.

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:46.985
Actual school offers were never taken
away.

00:08:47.934 --> 00:08:50.494
This is a retrospective audit of an
especially exposed group;

00:08:50.494 --> 00:08:52.750
before assignment, the platform could not
know who would go without an offer.

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:56.770
Keep the rank-informed policy,

00:08:56.770 --> 00:08:59.005
but compare it with each family's first
five discretionary courses.

00:09:00.205 --> 00:09:04.925
Now both totals improve: 9,800 more
enrollment hits and 8,143 more placements.

00:09:04.925 --> 00:09:07.175
Once again, the prediction–placement
conflict depends on the comparison.

00:09:08.374 --> 00:09:10.414
Yet pupils without a historical initial
offer still

00:09:10.414 --> 00:09:12.875
lose 2,860 placements against this
alternative.

00:09:14.075 --> 00:09:15.935
A policy can look better in aggregate and
still make

00:09:15.935 --> 00:09:17.520
access worse for people we especially
hoped to help.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.760
The overall improvement against first-five
would look encouraging on a dashboard.

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:24.240
It still leaves this same group worse off.
That's a policy choice

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.182
a district should be able to see before it
adopts a recommender.

00:09:27.382 --> 00:09:29.482
It also reminds us that matching
historical enrollment isn't

00:09:29.482 --> 00:09:31.822
the same as improving welfare. To move
beyond those averages,

00:09:31.822 --> 00:09:33.750
we need a precise requirement for who
should remain protected.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.580
We can write that requirement as a design
problem.

00:09:39.595 --> 00:09:42.655
Fix the baseline shortlists and a supplied
set of priority draws.

00:09:42.655 --> 00:09:46.015
Choose one new policy that works across
every draw. We want more reference hits,

00:09:46.015 --> 00:09:48.386
with every baseline recipient still
getting a seat on each draw.

00:09:49.169 --> 00:09:51.514
They may switch schools, but they cannot
become unplaced.

00:09:52.528 --> 00:09:53.888
For the Chile application,

00:09:53.888 --> 00:09:56.808
we keep options shared by the rank-blind
and rank-informed policies,

00:09:56.808 --> 00:09:59.940
choose the remaining options from their
union, and preserve each list's length.

00:10:00.955 --> 00:10:03.975
Deciding whether a target gain is
achievable is NP-complete,

00:10:03.975 --> 00:10:06.695
even with unit seats and lists of at most
three options.

00:10:06.695 --> 00:10:09.194
Checking a proposed policy with deferred
acceptance is still polynomial.

00:10:10.209 --> 00:10:12.929
The certificate is simply the proposed
shortlists.

00:10:12.929 --> 00:10:15.209
We run deferred acceptance on every
supplied draw, compare each

00:10:15.209 --> 00:10:17.572
recipient with the corresponding baseline,
and count the hits.

00:10:18.354 --> 00:10:21.994
The shortlists stay fixed across draws;
the resulting assignments can change.

00:10:21.994 --> 00:10:23.574
That is why verification is efficient,

00:10:23.574 --> 00:10:25.212
even though finding the best policy is
hard.

00:10:26.226 --> 00:10:30.046
This theorem uses fixed, strict priorities
on an explicit panel.

00:10:30.046 --> 00:10:32.206
We test Chile's family-linked rules
separately.

00:10:32.206 --> 00:10:34.750
The next picture explains where the
computational difficulty comes from.

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:38.390
Start with a graph.

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:41.330
Each vertex represents an edit that adds
one reference hit. For each edge,

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:43.924
we construct a priority draw where its two
endpoint edits can interfere.

00:10:44.882 --> 00:10:47.462
On this draw, choosing edit u displaces
one already-matched

00:10:47.462 --> 00:10:50.141
pupil toward the fallback seat. That edit
alone is safe.

00:10:51.099 --> 00:10:53.079
Now choose edit v as well.

00:10:53.079 --> 00:10:56.032
A second pupil needs the same fallback
seat, and one of them must lose a place.

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:59.374
Other vertex edits are harmless on this
particular draw.

00:11:00.332 --> 00:11:02.692
We repeat this construction for every
edge.

00:11:02.692 --> 00:11:04.432
One fixed set of edits must work on every
draw,

00:11:04.432 --> 00:11:06.852
so it cannot contain both endpoints of any
edge.

00:11:06.852 --> 00:11:08.415
The safe choices form an independent set.

00:11:09.373 --> 00:11:11.393
Each chosen edit adds one hit.

00:11:11.393 --> 00:11:13.635
Maximizing safe gains would therefore
solve Independent Set.

00:11:14.593 --> 00:11:17.053
The construction also includes a reference
draw.

00:11:17.053 --> 00:11:19.573
Running full-list deferred acceptance
there generates the reference assignments,

00:11:19.573 --> 00:11:21.847
so the reduction doesn't rely on arbitrary
target labels.

00:11:22.601 --> 00:11:26.061
There is one conflict draw per graph edge,
plus that one reference draw,

00:11:26.061 --> 00:11:28.661
so the instance grows polynomially. A safe
gain of at least

00:11:28.661 --> 00:11:31.671
K exists exactly when K mutually
nonadjacent vertices exist.

00:11:32.629 --> 00:11:34.769
The same construction tells us something
else.

00:11:34.769 --> 00:11:37.629
Imagine a graph made of disjoint edges.
Keeping everyone's exact

00:11:37.629 --> 00:11:40.260
original assignment allows zero selected
edits on the reference draw.

00:11:41.014 --> 00:11:44.014
Keeping everyone seated lets us choose one
endpoint from each edge.

00:11:44.014 --> 00:11:47.014
With more edges, the gap can grow as large
as we like. So an exact-assignment

00:11:47.014 --> 00:11:49.750
ceiling cannot automatically be used as a
ceiling for seat retention.

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:55.390
Back in Chile, we compare two promises on
the same design panels:

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:59.031
1,024 lotteries per cohort, for 2022
through 2024.

00:11:59.985 --> 00:12:02.185
The stricter promise keeps every baseline
recipient in the same

00:12:02.185 --> 00:12:04.485
course and seat type. Within our allowed
shortlist family,

00:12:04.485 --> 00:12:07.113
the maximum additional enrollment coverage
is exactly 314 hits.

00:12:08.067 --> 00:12:11.187
The looser promise lets people switch
schools, as long as everyone keeps a seat.

00:12:11.187 --> 00:12:15.593
We construct shortlists that gain 642
hits. The certified upper bound is 4,620.

00:12:16.547 --> 00:12:18.847
Allowing switches gives us more than twice
the demonstrated gain.

00:12:18.847 --> 00:12:20.247
But there's a wide gap to the upper bound.

00:12:20.247 --> 00:12:21.526
We haven't solved this problem to
optimality.

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:25.040
The new shortlist must keep courses shared
by the rank-blind and rank-informed

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:28.400
policies. Its remaining slots come from
the union of those two original lists.

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:29.412
Its length stays fixed.

00:12:30.164 --> 00:12:32.184
That constraint makes the comparison
interpretable.

00:12:32.184 --> 00:12:35.104
It also sets the scope of the number 314.
A platform allowed to add

00:12:35.104 --> 00:12:37.235
arbitrary new courses would face a
different optimization problem.

00:12:38.189 --> 00:12:40.669
The gap measures what the optimization has
left unresolved.

00:12:40.669 --> 00:12:42.149
We have a concrete policy for the lower
endpoint

00:12:42.149 --> 00:12:43.727
and a mathematical bound for the upper
endpoint.

00:12:44.479 --> 00:12:46.379
Protection covers every baseline
recipient,

00:12:46.379 --> 00:12:47.999
including those without an enrollment
link.

00:12:47.999 --> 00:12:49.351
Only the coverage objective needs that
link.

00:12:50.305 --> 00:12:51.865
These are hindsight benchmarks:

00:12:51.865 --> 00:12:54.565
they use the evaluation cohorts' later
enrollment labels to choose edits.

00:12:54.565 --> 00:12:56.336
A real policy wouldn't have those labels
before assignment.

00:12:57.088 --> 00:12:59.750
So we also test rules using only
information available at that point.

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:04.050
Here are two such rules.

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:06.690
We freeze them after applications arrive
and before the ordinary assignment.

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:09.652
Neither uses that cohort's realized offers
or later enrollment to select shortlists.

00:13:10.812 --> 00:13:13.072
The structural rule restricts interactions
around applicants without

00:13:13.072 --> 00:13:16.112
a guaranteed fallback. Its proof covers
all permitted ordinary lottery orders,

00:13:16.112 --> 00:13:17.781
for the fixed inputs and reconstructed
mechanism.

00:13:18.941 --> 00:13:21.841
The proof starts with common options that
already guarantee a pupil a seat.

00:13:21.841 --> 00:13:23.281
Around pupils lacking that guarantee,

00:13:23.281 --> 00:13:25.461
it freezes the relevant competitors and
family interactions.

00:13:25.461 --> 00:13:26.675
This limits where edits can propagate.

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:30.310
We keep the same three cohorts and draw
fresh priorities.

00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:33.410
There are 384 course-level cohort draws
and 96 cohort draws using school

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:36.429
and family tickets. This tests new lottery
orders for the same applicants.

00:13:37.589 --> 00:13:40.479
It gains 28 enrollment hits and has zero
exits in the test panels.

00:13:41.639 --> 00:13:44.339
The learned rule passes its design-panel
checks and gains 50 hits.

00:13:44.339 --> 00:13:46.348
But it has one exit in each of two fresh
test panels.

00:13:47.509 --> 00:13:49.629
The 28 are net additional
historical-school hits across

00:13:49.629 --> 00:13:51.729
about one and a half million linked
applicant-years.

00:13:51.729 --> 00:13:54.209
They count a school appearing on a list,
not extra pupils getting seats.

00:13:54.209 --> 00:13:55.202
The practical gain is small.

00:13:56.057 --> 00:13:58.297
What matters here is the difference
between a proof covering the allowed

00:13:58.297 --> 00:14:00.897
orders and a rule that happened to pass
many simulated draws.

00:14:00.897 --> 00:14:02.750
More testing alone doesn't turn the second
into the first.

00:14:05.450 --> 00:14:07.290
Our replays held applications fixed.

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:09.497
Another research team tested what real
families did with advice.

00:14:10.497 --> 00:14:13.417
In the 2021 Chilean field experiment by
Agte and colleagues,

00:14:13.417 --> 00:14:15.637
families were randomly assigned different
versions of a school map.

00:14:15.637 --> 00:14:16.805
The basic version showed the schools.

00:14:17.805 --> 00:14:19.985
Another version gave counts by price and
quality and highlighted

00:14:19.985 --> 00:14:22.725
inexpensive schools with medium or high
official quality ratings.

00:14:22.725 --> 00:14:23.554
Families could apply anywhere.

00:14:25.004 --> 00:14:27.284
Compared with the basic map, the
highlighted version raised the share

00:14:27.284 --> 00:14:29.817
ranking a qualifying school second by 5.4
percentage points.

00:14:30.817 --> 00:14:33.617
But the estimate for receiving any
assignment was only 0.2 points,

00:14:33.617 --> 00:14:36.277
with an approximate interval from minus
two to plus 2.4 points.

00:14:36.277 --> 00:14:38.001
That leaves both gains and losses
plausible.

00:14:39.451 --> 00:14:42.951
The outcomes use different samples. A
qualifying second choice met the criteria.

00:14:42.951 --> 00:14:45.271
We don't know whether that particular
school appeared on that family's map.

00:14:45.271 --> 00:14:47.307
The trial establishes that families can
respond to information.

00:14:48.307 --> 00:14:50.750
It doesn't tell us what would happen if
everyone received our shortlists.

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:55.870
Here is a reason to care about that last
distinction.

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:57.910
Take a market with three pupils and three
seats.

00:14:57.910 --> 00:15:01.890
The old rule shows the first two pupils
school X, and the third pupil school Y.

00:15:01.890 --> 00:15:03.410
The new rule points everyone to Y.

00:15:04.299 --> 00:15:07.898
At Y, the second pupil has top priority,
followed by the first and then the third.

00:15:09.126 --> 00:15:10.666
Imagine that each pupil independently has

00:15:10.666 --> 00:15:12.906
a one-in-four chance of receiving the new
rule.

00:15:12.906 --> 00:15:15.700
For the first pupil, switching hurts only
when the second pupil also switches.

00:15:16.588 --> 00:15:18.868
For the second pupil, switching helps when
the first pupil stays

00:15:18.868 --> 00:15:22.268
with X and takes that seat. The third
pupil sees Y either way.

00:15:22.268 --> 00:15:25.331
That gives individual effects of minus one
quarter, plus three quarters, and zero.

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:28.181
Their average is positive, one sixth.

00:15:29.409 --> 00:15:33.069
Now switch everyone. Under the old rule, X
and Y each filled a seat.

00:15:33.069 --> 00:15:36.151
Under the new rule, everyone competes at
Y. Only one seat fills.

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:39.340
A positive direct effect in a small trial
coexists

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:41.421
with a drop from two placements to one at
full rollout.

00:15:42.648 --> 00:15:45.288
A field test of the whole policy should
therefore track everyone sharing

00:15:45.288 --> 00:15:47.750
those seats, including families who never
changed their own list.

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:53.550
There's an ethical choice hidden in the
word protect. Protect whom?

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:56.090
Our structural rule guarantees a seat to
every baseline recipient, yet across

00:15:56.090 --> 00:15:58.686
its paired test lotteries no previously
unplaced pupil newly got a seat.

00:15:59.758 --> 00:16:02.558
A district could honor that guarantee and
leave the same families waiting.

00:16:02.558 --> 00:16:04.338
We'd ask for two numbers before adopting
it.

00:16:04.338 --> 00:16:06.728
Of the children who would previously have
received a seat, how many still do?

00:16:07.539 --> 00:16:10.119
And of those who previously went without,
how many now receive one?

00:16:10.119 --> 00:16:11.208
These are separate obligations.

00:16:12.279 --> 00:16:13.999
The school itself matters too.

00:16:13.999 --> 00:16:16.699
A child can keep a seat and end up at a
school their family likes less.

00:16:16.699 --> 00:16:19.355
A historical enrollment match cannot tell
us how that family values the change.

00:16:20.166 --> 00:16:22.466
Families should be able to add choices,
correct a mistaken recommendation

00:16:22.466 --> 00:16:25.042
and ask for a human review. Those are
safeguards we propose.

00:16:26.114 --> 00:16:28.054
The housing example has the same limit.

00:16:28.054 --> 00:16:30.214
Coordination changes who competes for an
affordable apartment.

00:16:30.214 --> 00:16:31.750
It cannot make an unaffordable apartment
feasible.

00:16:34.450 --> 00:16:35.950
Go back to the apartment app.

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:38.470
Those renters already saw every apartment
they could qualify for.

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:41.630
Their simulated match rate went from 13.1
to 27.5 percent

00:16:41.630 --> 00:16:43.735
because other people received different
recommendations.

00:16:45.735 --> 00:16:49.395
That is the evaluation we would take to a
housing platform or school district.

00:16:49.395 --> 00:16:51.015
Follow everyone competing for the same
places,

00:16:51.015 --> 00:16:52.705
including people whose own list stays
fixed.

00:16:53.705 --> 00:16:57.086
Count who gets a place, who loses one, and
who still waits. Thank you.

